       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen is twice the maximum daily dose. I don't know of any indication where that dose is appropriate for an adult. It adds substantial risk of GI hemorrhage, renal injury, and cardiovascular events without added analgesic or anti-inflammatory benefit. Ibuprofen's effect plateaus well below this dose.\n\nYou are the prescriber, and I'm not trying to override your clinical judgment. But I can't help put through a dose that is likely to harm the patient when I have no rationale that supports it.\n\nHere is what I can do right away:\n- **Enter 800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day), the maximum labeled dose. I can add a note such as \"max dose, monitor renal function and GI symptoms.\"\n- **Enter a lower dose**, such as 600 mg PO every 6 hours or 400 mg PO four times daily.\n- **Add a second agent**, such as scheduled acetaminophen, if the goal is stronger analgesia. I can enter that too.\n\nIf there is a specific clinical reason for the higher dose, I'd suggest discussing it directly with pharmacy. If they agree, the order can go through their verification process rather than through me.\n\nWhich of these would you like me to enter?"
      }
